  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 1211 AM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nAn upper-level shortwave trough will move from the Desert Southwest \ninto south Texas on Wednesday and Thursday of this week then to the \ngulf coast on Friday.  Southeasterly surface flow is expected for \nWednesday through Friday which should provide warm conditions across \nthe region.  850mb temperatures will be around 17-18 deg C so highs \nshould reach the mid to upper 80s each afternoon. Some locations in \nthe Big Country will reach 90. A few afternoon showers could occur \nalong and near the Interstate-10 corridor Thursday night into Friday \nbut these will be fairly spotty so PoPs were kept under 20 percent \nduring this time.\n\nFor the weekend, global models have come into better agreement of a \npotential tropical system developing and moving northward out of the \ngulf into the Mississippi Delta region. Regardless of the strength \nof this system it should have little to no impact on the weather for \nwest central Texas.  However, the aforementioned upper-level trough \nlooks to be absorbed into this tropical system as it makes landfall. \nIn its wake, flow aloft should become southwesterly and allow for \nmostly warm and dry conditions to continue with above-normal \ntemperatures.  Chances for rain look to increase for the early to \nmiddle part of next week as a much larger-scale trough digs in \nacross the western US.",
